{"causes":["Alaric's Visigoth invasion of Rome in 410 CE led to the city's transformation into a Christian entity","The rise of Christianity in the Roman Empire during the 4th century CE created a new cultural and political landscape","The decline of the Roman Empire's power and influence in the Western world led to a reevaluation of the concept of the 'City of God'"],"effects":["Shaped Western Christianity's understanding of salvation and the nature of God","Influenced the development of medieval thought and the rise of scholasticism","Continues to be relevant in contemporary debates about the nature of God and salvation"],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"St. Augustine of Hippo","sourceName":"St. Augustine of Hippo","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"The City of God","targetName":"The City of God","context":"In response to the sacking of Rome by Alaric's Visigoths in 410 CE"},{"sourceSlug":"Alaric","sourceName":"Alaric","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"The City of God","targetName":"The City of God","context":"Caused the city's transformation into a Christian entity"},{"sourceSlug":"Thomas Aquinas","sourceName":"Thomas Aquinas","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"The City of God","targetName":"The City of God","context":"In his Summa Theologica"},{"sourceSlug":"Martin Luther","sourceName":"Martin Luther","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"The City of God","targetName":"The City of God","context":"In his Ninety-Five Theses"},{"sourceSlug":"The Roman Empire","sourceName":"The Roman Empire","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"The City of God","targetName":"The City of God","context":"Led to a reevaluation of the concept of the 'City of God'"},{"sourceSlug":"The Visigoths","sourceName":"The Visigoths","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"The City of God","targetName":"The City of God","context":"Caused the city's transformation into a Christian entity"}],"places":[{"name":"Rome","role":"Site of the sacking"},{"name":"Hippo","role":"Place of St. Augustine's residence"},{"name":"Jerusalem","role":"Place of influence for Christian theology"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: The City of God","type":"Reference article","slug":"the-city-of-god"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q212318","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_City_of_God"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"The City of God contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The City of God Against the Pagans (Latin: De Civitate Dei contra Paganos) is a major philosophical and theological work by Augustine of Hippo (354–430 CE), Bishop of Hippo Regius in North Africa, composed c. 413–426 CE in response to the sack of Rome by Alaric's Visigoths in 410 CE — an event that shocked the Roman world and prompted accusations that Christianity's abolition of the pagan cults was responsible for Rome's military weakness. In 22 books, Augustine develops a comprehensive philosophy of history and a theological account of the two 'cities' (civitates) — the City of God (civitas Dei, the community of those who love God above all things, including the angels and the predestined elect among humans) and the City of Man (civitas terrena, the community of those who love self above God, including the fallen angels and the damned) — which exist intermingled in earthly history but will be separated at the Last Judgement.

The City of God performs multiple tasks: Books I–X refute the pagan argument that Rome's adoption of Christianity caused its military collapse, reviewing Rome's entire history of military defeats and showing that pagan religion never guaranteed Roman security; Books XI–XXII develop Augustine's positive theology of history, the nature of the two cities, the fall of the angels, the fall of Adam, human free will and divine predestination, the City of God's pilgrimage through earthly history, and the eschatological culmination in the Last Judgement and the beatific vision. The City of God is both a massive work of Christian apologetics and the first systematic philosophy of history in the Western tradition.

Augustine's influence on subsequent Christian thought — on the theology of grace and predestination that shaped the debates of the Reformation (Luther and Calvin drew heavily on Augustine's anti-Pelagian writings, which are closely related to the City of God's theology), on medieval political theology (the distinction between spiritual and temporal authority), and on the philosophy of history (the idea of history as a meaningful narrative directed toward an eschatological goal) — is unparalleled among Western theologians.","subjects":["Christian Theology","Late Antique Era","Augustine of Hippo","Philosophy of History","Christian Apologetics","Latin Literature","Political Theology","Church Fathers"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The City of God's ideas about the nature of God and salvation have had a lasting impact on Western Christianity and continue to influence contemporary debates about these topics.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
